WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:03.825
A residents’ association discusses a shared courtyard.

00:04.209 --> 00:08.469
Longstanding owners speak freely, while several tenants stay quiet.

00:08.877 --> 00:11.033
The chair thinks they are uninterested.

00:11.428 --> 00:15.191
Salma wanted to ask about children playing there, but did not know

00:15.203 --> 00:16.581
whether tenants could vote.

00:16.964 --> 00:19.196
Another tenant had to leave for work.

00:19.591 --> 00:23.061
Someone else feared that criticism might reach the landlord.

00:24.080 --> 00:28.429
Before judging silence, ask what makes speaking easy or risky here.

00:28.800 --> 00:32.588
Culture includes shared customs and ideas about politeness,

00:32.843 --> 00:34.667
responsibility and belonging.

00:35.063 --> 00:38.022
Families and workplaces have customs too.

00:38.391 --> 00:42.371
But a broad description of a culture cannot tell you what Salma thinks.

00:42.754 --> 00:46.160
Ask about this meeting rather than expecting her to speak for

00:46.173 --> 00:47.793
everyone from her background.

00:48.830 --> 00:54.009
A descriptive norm concerns what people commonly do, or believe others commonly do.

00:54.418 --> 00:59.035
A prescriptive norm concerns what people believe others expect or approve of.

00:59.418 --> 01:02.760
Newcomers usually stay quiet describes a pattern.

01:03.142 --> 01:07.288
Newcomers are expected to stay quiet describes an expectation.

01:07.696 --> 01:11.854
Those are different claims, even when both could be true in the same group.

01:12.872 --> 01:15.206
The chair may say everyone can speak.

01:15.590 --> 01:20.322
Members also notice whether questions are welcomed, interrupted or mocked.

01:20.691 --> 01:24.020
Compare the stated rule with what happens in practice.

01:24.403 --> 01:28.396
Notice who receives a different response and who already knows how to put

01:28.408 --> 01:29.658
an item on the agenda.

01:30.028 --> 01:33.165
Confidence alone does not explain who gets heard.

01:34.205 --> 01:39.422
Sociologist Annette Lareau’s observations of family life examined how social class

01:39.435 --> 01:42.241
related to children’s dealings with organisations.

01:42.624 --> 01:47.165
Familiar ways of asking and negotiating could help people obtain support.

01:47.548 --> 01:49.933
This does not classify every family.

01:50.341 --> 01:54.372
In the residents’ example, explaining voting rights and how to submit

01:54.385 --> 01:56.196
questions may help newcomers.

01:56.591 --> 02:01.017
Those are practical suggestions, not outcomes established by that study.

02:02.038 --> 02:06.362
One resident may hear an invitation as helping with maintenance once.

02:06.746 --> 02:10.368
Another may hear it as taking responsibility every week.

02:10.777 --> 02:13.315
Watching attendance would miss that difference.

02:13.723 --> 02:17.371
Explain the commitment and ask what people understand it to mean.

02:17.766 --> 02:22.843
Research across societies also cautions against assuming that a familiar arrangement

02:22.856 --> 02:24.437
has the same meaning everywhere.

02:25.455 --> 02:30.519
Ask who controls the agenda, budget and keys; who carries the costs; and who

02:30.532 --> 02:31.807
can challenge a decision.

02:32.177 --> 02:34.932
These questions do not produce a power score.

02:35.341 --> 02:37.828
They identify practical differences.

02:38.198 --> 02:43.172
Telling a tenant to speak up does not remove a risk to housing or essential support.

02:43.542 --> 02:47.037
Do not pressure someone to raise a concern publicly when doing

02:47.050 --> 02:48.568
so may put them at risk.

02:49.580 --> 02:54.185
Suppose Salma submits a question in advance and the chair publishes an answer.

02:54.594 --> 02:57.170
That shows the route worked for one concern.

02:57.578 --> 03:00.780
It does not show that everyone feels safe to disagree.

03:01.149 --> 03:05.180
Clarifying that only some residents can vote may reveal an unfair

03:05.192 --> 03:07.106
arrangement rather than fix it.

03:07.489 --> 03:10.920
Transparency lets the group discuss the rule honestly.

03:11.955 --> 03:16.266
When someone stays quiet or does not join in, ask: What makes taking

03:16.279 --> 03:17.924
part easy or risky here?

03:18.295 --> 03:22.006
Check information, expectations and consequences.

03:22.415 --> 03:24.341
Notice your own position too.

03:24.724 --> 03:28.691
A meeting may feel easy to you because you do not face the same risks.

03:29.073 --> 03:32.173
Improve one arrangement where you can, then check whose

03:32.186 --> 03:34.430
participation it actually supports.
